# MCSP Semantic Definitions — 0.6

**Status:** Constructive implementation  
**Qualification:** Under Conditional Experiment  
**Objective:** Reduce semantic residue toward a finite self-describing relation graph.

## Foundational representation

Every MCSP entity is provisionally a CONSTRUCT. Every formal association between constructs is a RELATION. Change is represented by TRANSITION. Distinguishable condition at an ordering point is STATE.

    CONSTRUCT {
      IDENTITY
      RELATION*
      STATE*
    }

    RELATION {
      source
      target
      kind
      STATE*
    }

    TRANSITION {
      pre_state
      OPERATION
      CONSTRAINT*
      post_state
      OUTCOME
    }

These forms are themselves constructs and relations.

## Definition

    DEFINE is a CONSTRUCT whose RELATION set identifies:
      identity
      kind
      inputs
      outputs
      preconditions
      transition
      outcomes
      relations
      realization_constraints

No DEFINE field is privileged syntax at the semantic layer.

## Residue reduction

### SCOPE

    DEFINE SCOPE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: identity-set, containing-construct
      outputs: identity-domain
      transition: relate identities to a domain in which distinction is evaluated
    }

IDENTITY is distinction within SCOPE.

### ORDER

    DEFINE ORDER {
      kind: relation
      inputs: constructs
      outputs: precedence-relations
      transition: relate constructs by before/equal/after positions in an ordering space
    }

Temporal order is one realization of ORDER; MCSP does not require physical wall-clock time.

### OBSERVE

    DEFINE OBSERVE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: construct, state, ordering-position
      outputs: observation
      transition: relate distinguishable state to an ordering position
    }

An observer as an anthropomorphic entity is not required. Observation means state made distinguishable at a relation point.

### REPRESENT

    DEFINE REPRESENT {
      kind: relation
      inputs: source-construct, target-state, mapping
      outputs: representation
      transition: establish correspondence by which target-state distinguishes source-construct
    }

Representation is therefore a relation, not a privileged byte/string form.

### UNIT

    DEFINE UNIT {
      kind: construct
      inputs: identity, partition-relation
      outputs: unit
      transition: establish a repeatable partition reference within a space
    }

### EXTENT

    DEFINE EXTENT {
      kind: relation
      inputs: start-position, end-position, unit
      outputs: extent
      transition: relate bounded positions under a unit
    }

### COORDINATE

    DEFINE COORDINATE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: position, space, reference-position, extent-relations
      outputs: coordinate
      transition: represent position relative to a reference within space
    }

Coordinates are representations of POSITION, not POSITION itself.

### MAP

    DEFINE MAP {
      kind: relation
      inputs: source-space, target-space, correspondence-set
      outputs: mapping
      transition: relate source positions/constructs to target positions/constructs
    }

TRANSLATE applies a MAP to derive a target position.

### REQUIRE

    DEFINE REQUIRE {
      kind: constraint
      inputs: construct-or-transition, admissible-state-relation
      outputs: requirement
      transition: restrict admissible state/transition set
    }

### EVENT

    DEFINE EVENT {
      kind: construct
      inputs: transition, observation
      outputs: event
      transition: represent an observed transition relation
    }

### RULE

    DEFINE RULE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: admissible-pre-state, operation, constraints, admissible-post-state
      outputs: rule
      transition: relate admissible transition components
    }

A RULE does not execute itself. OPERATION may reference a RULE; TRANSITION records an occurrence/effect.

### CAUSE

    DEFINE CAUSE {
      kind: relation
      inputs: antecedent-event-or-state, consequent-event-or-state, rule
      outputs: cause-relation
      transition: relate antecedent and consequent under an explicit rule
    }

This avoids treating causality as an unexplained universal physical assertion; a cause claim is scoped to a declared rule/model.

### AVAILABLE

    DEFINE AVAILABLE {
      kind: state
      inputs: resource-or-capability, scope, constraints
      outputs: availability-state
      transition: classify whether allocation/realization is admissible in scope
    }

### LOGICAL OBSERVATION POSITION

No new primitive is introduced.

    logical_observation_position :=
      POSITION within an ORDER space at which OBSERVE relates STATE

This removes the 0.5 compound residue term.

## Width and value refinement

    WIDTH := EXTENT over a representation space under UNIT

    VALUE := a CONSTRUCT distinguished by REPRESENT relations and bounded by WIDTH where applicable

Bits, bytes, words, vector lanes, sectors and protocol fields are realization/domain units rather than assumed universal units.

## Address refinement

    ADDRESS := REPRESENT(POSITION within address-space)

An address is therefore not identical to a position. It is a representation of a position under a mapping/space model.

    TRANSLATE(address/position, MAP) -> target POSITION

Virtual, physical, bus, device and protocol address forms can use this model without being conflated.

## Scheduling refinement

    SCHEDULE {
      transitions
      ORDER
      CONSTRAINT*
    } -> admissible execution-order

Scheduling is therefore an ORDER relation constrained over TRANSITION constructs.

## Allocation refinement

    ALLOCATE(resource-space, EXTENT, CONSTRAINT*)
      -> RELATION(construct, available region/resource)

    RELEASE(allocation-relation)
      -> transition terminating that relation

Allocation does not imply ownership, execution or persistence unless separate relations establish them.

## Outcome refinement

    OUTCOME := STATE classification related to TRANSITION

Initial classifications remain:
    completed
    exception
    unsupported
    implementation-defined
    environment-defined
    incomplete

Classification names are constructs/identities, not host exception encodings.

## Realization refinement

    REALIZATION :=
      MAP from MCSP semantic constructs/relations
      to host constructs/relations
      under CAPABILITY and CONSTRAINT relations

A valid realization must preserve the declared semantic relation. It may expand one MCSP operation into multiple host operations.

## Recursive closure graph

Current candidate graph:

    CONSTRUCT
      -> IDENTITY
          -> SCOPE
              -> RELATION
      -> RELATION
          -> CONSTRUCT
      -> STATE
          -> OBSERVE
              -> ORDER
                  -> RELATION
      -> TRANSITION
          -> OPERATION
              -> RULE
                  -> RELATION
          -> CONSTRAINT
              -> REQUIRE
                  -> RELATION
          -> OUTCOME
              -> STATE

The graph is finite structurally, but semantic circularity remains under examination.

## Circularity findings

### Construct / relation cycle

CONSTRUCT and RELATION refer to one another. This is not automatically failure: a graph model may be mutually recursive. Closure requires that their distinction be representable without importing an undefined external semantic category.

### State / observation cycle

STATE was previously defined through observations while OBSERVE refers to state. 0.6 therefore weakens STATE to:

    STATE := distinguishable configuration relations of a CONSTRUCT at an ORDER position

OBSERVE records/accesses that state; it no longer creates the definition of STATE.

### Identity / scope cycle

IDENTITY depends on SCOPE and SCOPE contains identities. Treat SCOPE as a relation domain; identity distinction is evaluated under that relation. Further formal testing is required.

## 0.6 residue

Remaining semantic questions are reduced to:

- distinguishability
- configuration
- set/cardinality
- partition
- correspondence
- admissibility
- occurrence
- preservation/equivalence of semantic relations
- primitive graph termination criterion

These are deeper mathematical/logical concepts. They must not be silently declared machine primitives merely to force closure.

## 0.6 qualification

**PASS:** 0.5 residue terms representation, observation, unit, extent, scope, coordinates, mapping, ordering, requirement, event, rule, cause, logical observation position and availability now have explicit MCSP decompositions.

**PASS:** ADDRESS is separated from POSITION; WIDTH is reduced to EXTENT; scheduling is reduced to ORDER + CONSTRAINT.

**PASS:** a state/observation definitional cycle was identified and reduced.

**OPEN:** finite semantic closure has moved to a smaller foundational residue involving distinction, configuration, collection/partition, correspondence, admissibility and semantic preservation.

**Qualification remains:** Under Conditional Experiment.
